{"id":"xpo-9aff89","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-dd0fc9f3-aa02-43c1-b6c7-320c7db58e26","text":"## Summary\n\nMultiple beats board instances can now run simultaneously across different projects. Each instance picks a free port if the requested one is taken, registers itself in a shared file-based registry, and the sidebar lists every running instance as a first-class entry so the user can jump between projects with a single click.\n\n## Coordination model: peer registry (no master)\n\nConsidered three approaches:\n\n1. **Master proxy** — first instance binds 8080 and reverse-proxies path-based to follower ports. Rejected: complex (path routing, asset rewriting, websockets), and killing the master loses access to all projects.\n2. **Master registry, separate ports** — first instance owns a registry endpoint; followers register over HTTP. Rejected: followers go dark if master dies.\n3. **Peer registry (chosen)** — every instance is equal. All write/read `$TMPDIR/beats/instances.json` with atomic rename. Stale entries are pruned by PID-liveness check on every read.\n\nThe peer model has no special instance, survives any process dying, and avoids reverse-proxy plumbing entirely.\n\n## Backend\n\n### `internal/registry/registry.go` (new package)\n\n- `Entry { Name, Port, PID, RootDir, StartedAt }`\n- `Register(name, port, rootDir)` — loads, prunes dead PIDs and any existing entry for self, appends, writes atomically (tmp + `os.Rename`).\n- `UnregisterByPID(pid)` — loads, filters out, writes.\n- `List()` — loads, filters via `isAlive(pid)`, persists the cleaned list as a side effect. So even a hard-killed peer is auto-cleaned the next time anyone reads.\n- `FindFreePort(start, maxAttempts)` — loops `net.Listen` from start upward.\n- Liveness via `os.FindProcess(pid).Signal(syscall.Signal(0))`. Reliable on Unix; may produce false positives on Windows (documented).\n\n### `internal/server/server.go`\n\nSplit `Start()` into:\n\n- `Bind() (net.Listener, error)` — acquires the listener, retries up to 10 ports starting from the requested one, **writes the actual bound port back to `s.Port`**.\n- `ServeOn(listener)` — serves on the provided listener.\n- `Start()` kept as a Bind + ServeOn convenience for any non-board callers.\n\nBind/Serve separation matters because the caller needs the actual port (for browser-open, registry registration, log output) **before** `Serve` blocks.\n\n### `internal/server/handlers.go` + `router.go`\n\n- New `handleListInstances` returns the live list with an `is_current` flag per entry (matched by port).\n- Route: `GET /api/instances`.\n\n### `cmd/beats/board.go`\n\nLifecycle:\n\n1. `srv.Bind()` — get listener + actual port.\n2. Resolve project name: `cfg.Name` then `filepath.Base(cwd)`.\n3. `registry.Register(name, srv.Port, cwd)` — soft failure, logs a warning.\n4. `defer registry.UnregisterByPID(...)` for clean returns.\n5. SIGINT/SIGTERM goroutine: unregisters then `os.Exit(0)`.\n6. Open browser at the **actual** port (was hardcoded to the requested port before).\n7. `srv.ServeOn(listener)`.\n\nPort-collision log: `Port 8080 in use, starting on port 8081 instead`.\n\n## Frontend\n\n### `web/src/api/client.ts`\n\nNew `Instance` type and `fetchInstances()` against `/api/instances`.\n\n### `web/src/components/Layout/Layout.tsx`\n\n- Fetches instances on mount, polls every 10s, refreshes on `window.focus`.\n- New `ProjectItem` component that renders each instance as a first-class sidebar entry styled like a `NavItem` (folder icon + truncated name + muted `:port`).\n- Current instance renders as a non-interactive `\u003cdiv\u003e` with the active-item highlight (`bg-hover` + primary text + `font-medium`).\n- Peer instances are `\u003ca href=\"http://localhost:$port/\"\u003e` links — same-tab cross-origin navigation per agreed UX.\n- `title` attribute on each row exposes the full `root_dir` on hover so two same-named directories are distinguishable.\n- Section sits beneath the existing nav, separated by a `border-t` divider, headed by a small uppercase \"PROJECTS\" label.\n\n## Behaviour\n\n- One instance running → sidebar shows just that project (its own row, highlighted).\n- Two terminals running `beats board` in different dirs → each picks a free port, both register, each sidebar lists both rows with the local one highlighted and the peer clickable.\n- Clean Ctrl-C → entry removed from registry.\n- `kill -9` → entry remains until any peer next calls `List()`, then it's auto-pruned.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `go build ./...` — clean\n- `go test ./internal/...` — passes\n- `make build` — builds end-to-end\n- Manually tested: launched two boards in different projects, sidebar listed both, switching worked, clean shutdown unregistered correctly"},"created_at":"2026-05-26T09:33:47.287254798Z","created_by":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e"}
