{"id":"xpo-b75e16","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"status":"DOING"},"created_at":"2026-05-27T12:02:14.041703531Z","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg <nicbet@gmail.com>","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.152 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"xpo-b75e16","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-0126e049-5f30-47cd-a4c1-16d250f6e9b0","text":"## README rewrite — walkthrough\n\nReplaced the old design-doc-style README (event-sourcing rationale, future-tense CLI spec, SPA-fallback prose) with a user-facing document. The architectural deep-dive content was not duplicated to [design_docs/DESIGN.md](design_docs/DESIGN.md); it's still there if anyone needs it.\n\n### New structure\n\n1. **Title + tagline** — *\"The git-native issue tracker for humans and AI agents.\"* Followed by a 3-sentence \"what is it\" paragraph.\n2. **Why beats** — five bullets framing the value props: git-native, single binary, agent-first, event-sourced, local web UI.\n3. **Quickstart** — four-step path: `make \u0026\u0026 make install` → `beats init` → `beats add` → `beats board`.\n4. **Installation** — from source via `make \u0026\u0026 make install`, with Go 1.25+ and bun as prerequisites. Lists the other `make` targets (`cli`, `test`, `clean`).\n5. **CLI usage** — daily workflow, status transitions, filtering, relationships, and audit/maintenance, each as a small annotated code block.\n6. **Web UI** — what `beats board` does, the `Cmd/Ctrl` precise-slot and `Alt`-to-nest gestures, `Cmd/Ctrl+K` palette, localhost-only binding.\n7. **Agent integration (MCP)** — kept most of the existing section (it was already user-facing and good). Three setup paths for Claude Code, the seven-tool table, agent identity precedence, `BEATS_AGENT_IDENTITY` env var example.\n8. **Configuration** — precedence list, options table, example `config.yaml`.\n9. **Architecture (one paragraph)** — short summary, deep link to [design_docs/DESIGN.md](design_docs/DESIGN.md).\n10. **Building \u0026 contributing** — `make` targets, repo layout pointers.\n\n### Notable choices\n\n- **Tagline**: used \"issue tracker\" (correcting the apparent typo \"issuer\" in the request). Flag if you actually meant \"issuer\".\n- **Install path**: `make \u0026\u0026 make install` per request, with a note that `make install` will prompt for `sudo` because the Makefile copies to `/usr/local/bin/beats`.\n- **MCP section retained nearly verbatim**: that section was the one part of the old README that was already correctly aimed at users.\n- **No badges, no emoji, no marketing fluff** — kept the tone matter-of-fact to match the existing project voice.\n\n### Verification\n\nDoc-only change; nothing to test or build. Markdown is internally consistent (no unclosed code fences, all relative links resolve to files that exist in the repo).\n\nReady for review."},"created_at":"2026-05-27T12:06:24.86440707Z","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg <nicbet@gmail.com>","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.152 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"xpo-b75e16","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-0ef74b59-8e65-4a84-8fa5-1f624c1cf19c","text":"## Follow-up — added permission-allowlist section\n\nAdded a new `### Reducing permission prompts` subsection under \"Agent integration (MCP)\", between the tools table and the agent-identity section. Shows two `.claude/settings.json` patterns:\n\n1. **Wildcard** — `\"mcp__beats__beats_*\"` allows every beats MCP tool with no prompt.\n2. **Per-tool list** — explicit entries for the seven `beats_*` tools, useful if you want to auto-allow reads but keep writes prompted.\n\nIncludes a one-line rationale: all beats writes are append-only events on a git-tracked file, so an erroneous call is recoverable."},"created_at":"2026-05-27T12:08:50.079815114Z","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg <nicbet@gmail.com>","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.152 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"xpo-b75e16","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-2420df6b-215c-4763-951a-4de2ffb180c3","text":"## Follow-up — added multi-instance paragraph to Web UI section\n\nInserted a short paragraph between the UI gestures bullet list and the `127.0.0.1` security note, documenting the multi-project behavior from [xpo-9aff89](#):\n\n- Port 8080 collision → auto-fallback to next free port.\n- Multiple `beats board` instances across projects discover each other via a shared local registry; the sidebar lists peers as one-click navigation entries.\n- Clean shutdowns auto-unregister; hard-killed instances are pruned on the next peer refresh.\n\nKept it to three sentences so it doesn't dominate the Web UI section."},"created_at":"2026-05-27T12:12:45.973647214Z","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg <nicbet@gmail.com>","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.152 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
