{"id":"xpo-075586","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"Hosted Mode: multi-user beats server with SSH key auth","description":"## Vision\n\nTurn beats into a self-hosted project management server — a single binary that works equally well in local (CLI) and remote (hosted) deployments without friction.\n\nIn hosted mode, beats runs on a dedicated repository (e.g. `project-name-beats.git`) and serves both an MCP endpoint and the web UI over the network. Users authenticate with SSH keys and interact via standard MCP clients, the beats CLI (`RemoteClient`), or the browser.\n\n## Design\n\n### Transport: MCP over HTTP\n\n`beats serve` exposes two interfaces on a single port:\n\n- **MCP endpoint** (`/mcp`) — the same tool surface that `beats mcp` provides today (list, show, add, update, comment, link, history), served over HTTP instead of stdio. Any standard MCP client can connect with a Bearer token.\n- **Web UI** — the existing React app + REST API (`/api/*`), served to browsers with session-based auth.\n\nNo new API surface to design. The MCP tools already cover every operation; the REST API already powers the web UI. Both are thin layers over the same `beats.Client` and event-sourced storage.\n\n### Authentication: SSH key signatures → Bearer tokens\n\nUsers authenticate by proving ownership of an SSH private key. The server verifies against known public keys.\n\n**Flow:**\n\n1. User runs `beats login https://beats.myteam.dev`\n2. Server returns a nonce\n3. CLI signs the nonce with the user's SSH key (`ssh-keygen -Y sign` or Go's `golang.org/x/crypto/ssh`)\n4. Server verifies the signature against `.beats/keys` (or fallback `~/.ssh/authorized_keys`)\n5. Server returns a Bearer token (a signed blob containing the public key fingerprint + expiry)\n6. Token is cached locally and used for all subsequent requests\n\n**Token usage:**\n\n- **MCP clients** — `Authorization: Bearer \u003ctoken\u003e` in the MCP config headers. Works with any standard MCP client (Claude Code, Cursor, etc.) without custom transport code.\n- **Web UI** — `beats login` opens the browser with the session token set as a cookie. Short-lived, refreshable via CLI.\n- **RemoteClient** — attaches the token to every HTTP request automatically.\n\n**Example MCP client config:**\n\n```json\n{\n  \"mcpServers\": {\n    \"beats\": {\n      \"url\": \"https://beats.myteam.dev/mcp\",\n      \"headers\": {\n        \"Authorization\": \"Bearer \u003ctoken-from-xpo-login\u003e\"\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Key management\n\n- Primary: `.beats/keys` — a file listing authorized SSH public keys, one per line (same format as `authorized_keys`). Project-scoped, version-controlled with the beats data.\n- Fallback: `~/.ssh/authorized_keys` — convenience for single-user / personal setups.\n- Identity is derived from the public key → mapped to a user record. The verified identity flows into `Event.CreatedBy`.\n\n### `beats.RemoteClient`\n\nA new client implementation that speaks MCP JSON-RPC over HTTP instead of reading/writing the local JSONL file directly. The CLI detects whether it's configured for a remote server (via `beats remote add` or config) and uses `RemoteClient` transparently.\n\n- Same interface as the local `beats.Client` from the CLI's perspective\n- Serializes operations as MCP tool calls over HTTP\n- Attaches Bearer token to every request\n- Enables: `beats list --remote myproject`, `beats add \"Title\" --remote myproject`, etc.\n\n### `beats serve`\n\nA new subcommand that starts the hosted server:\n\n- Binds to a configurable address (default `0.0.0.0:8080`)\n- Serves MCP endpoint + Web UI + REST API on one port\n- Auth middleware validates Bearer tokens on all endpoints\n- TLS support (flag or auto via Let's Encrypt)\n- Periodic auto-commit + push to the git remote (the append-only JSONL makes this conflict-free)\n- Concurrency: SQLite WAL-style approach not needed — the JSONL append is naturally serializable; a write mutex suffices for small teams\n\n### Auto-backup to git\n\nSince the data lives in a dedicated repo and the storage is append-only JSONL:\n\n- Periodic `git add .beats/issues.db \u0026\u0026 git commit \u0026\u0026 git push` on a timer or after N writes\n- Diffs are always \"new lines appended\" — no merge conflicts\n- On startup, pull latest and rebuild the in-memory projection\n- The git repo is always the source of truth; if the server dies, anyone can clone and run beats locally against the snapshot\n\n### Separate repository model\n\nHosted beats runs against a dedicated repository (e.g. `project-name-beats.git`), not inside the project code repo. This avoids:\n\n- Polluting code history with issue-tracking commits\n- Branch/merge complications between code and issue data\n- Permission conflicts (issue access ≠ code access)\n\n## Stories (to be broken down later)\n\n- [ ] Extract `Client` interface / introduce `RemoteClient`\n- [ ] `beats serve` subcommand (network-bound MCP + Web UI)\n- [ ] SSH key auth: nonce signing, token issuance, `.beats/keys`\n- [ ] `beats login` CLI command\n- [ ] `beats remote add/remove` configuration\n- [ ] Auth middleware for MCP and REST endpoints\n- [ ] Auto-commit and push (periodic git backup)\n- [ ] TLS support\n- [ ] Web UI auth flow (CLI-assisted token → browser cookie)","status":"BACKLOG","labels":["epic","feature"]},"created_at":"2026-06-04T07:26:22.022546Z","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg <nicbet@gmail.com>","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.162 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"xpo-8c2171","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"When deleting a parent issue with sub-issues, the parent-id for the sub-issues is not unset for the sub-issues. Ideally, the WEB UI can ask the user if they want to a. unset the parent id from children, or b. delete all children together with the parent","labels":["bug"],"sort_order":"a9","title":"Parent issue id not existent"},"created_at":"2026-06-08T06:22:58.001306333Z","created_by":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e"}
