{"id":"xpo-3a219e","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"## Problem\n\nThe projection currently depends on event order in the JSONL file. Git merges can scramble line order — two clones that have seen the same set of events may compute different state depending on which order the lines ended up in after a merge. This breaks the convergence guarantee.\n\n## Solution\n\nEvents carry a **deterministic sort key** — `(timestamp, actor_id, event_id)` — and replay sorts by that key, not by file position.\n\nThen any two clones that have seen the same set of events compute the identical state. That's the convergence guarantee, and it's a pure function — no negotiation, no conflict resolution protocol.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Define a deterministic sort key: `(timestamp, actor_id, event_id)`\n- [ ] Projection replay sorts events by the sort key before applying them\n- [ ] Two JSONL files with the same events in different line order produce identical projected state\n- [ ] Existing events already have the fields needed for the sort key (verify, backfill if needed)\n- [ ] Add tests: shuffle event order, assert identical projection output","labels":["bug"],"status":"BACKLOG","title":"Replay has to be order independent"},"created_at":"2026-06-08T12:15:02.869112365Z","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg <nicbet@gmail.com>","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.168 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"xpo-c762f7","type":"CREATE","payload":{"dependencies":[{"kind":"relates_to","source_id":"xpo-c762f7","target_id":"xpo-3a219e"}],"description":"## Problem\n\nWhen two branches append events to `.beats/issues.db` concurrently, git produces conflict markers on merge. The file is append-only JSONL — both sides' lines are valid and should both survive — but git's default merge strategy doesn't know that.\n\n## Solution\n\nSet `merge=union` in `.gitattributes`:\n\n```\n.beats/issues.db merge=union\n```\n\nThis tells git to keep lines from both sides on a merge, which is exactly the right semantic for an append-only event log. No conflict markers, no manual resolution.\n\n### Alternative: per-event files\n\nStructurally impossible conflicts — each event is its own file (e.g. `.beats/events/\u003cevent-id\u003e.json`). Adding a file never conflicts with adding a different file. Trade-off: more filesystem overhead, changes to the storage layer. Worth considering long-term, but `merge=union` is the smaller change and solves the immediate problem.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `beats init` creates/updates `.gitattributes` with the `merge=union` rule for `issues.db`\n- [ ] Existing projects pick up the rule via `beats doctor` or `beats migrate`\n- [ ] Verify: two branches that each append events merge cleanly without conflict markers\n- [ ] Document the per-event-files alternative for future consideration\n\n## Related\n\nPairs with **xpo-3a219e** (order-independent replay) — union merge can interleave lines in any order, so replay must not depend on file position.","labels":["feature"],"status":"BACKLOG","title":"Non-blocking merge for issues.db via gitattributes"},"created_at":"2026-06-08T12:43:54.041100155Z","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg <nicbet@gmail.com>","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.168 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"xpo-f23cf5","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"## Overview\n\nExpand the event-sourced projection to use the **right merge type per field**, instead of naïve last-writer-wins on everything. The event log is already append-only and immutable — the change is in how projection folds events into state, and in adding new fine-grained event types for set-valued fields.\n\n## Field → Merge Strategy\n\n| Field | Merge type | Mechanism |\n|---|---|---|\n| `status` | LWW register | Sort-key tiebreak; state machine guards terminal states |\n| `assignee`, `priority`, `points`, `estimate` | LWW register | Conflicts rare, low-stakes |\n| `labels` | OR-Set | New `LABEL_ADD` / `LABEL_REMOVE` event types |\n| `comments` | Grow-only log | Already correct — no change needed |\n| `dependencies` / `links` | OR-Set | New `LINK_ADD` / `LINK_REMOVE` event types |\n| `description` | LWW register | Loser preserved in history; no collaborative-text CRDT |\n| `sort_order` | Fractional indexing | Already uses `GenerateNKeysBetween`; add actor tiebreak |\n\n## Design Decision: New Event Types\n\nNew event types (`LABEL_ADD`, `LABEL_REMOVE`, `LINK_ADD`, `LINK_REMOVE`) rather than delta fields on `UPDATE`. This gives us:\n\n- **Backwards compatibility for free** — old `UPDATE` events with full `labels`/`dependencies` arrays still project correctly as \"set to this value\"; new event types layer on top with finer-grained semantics.\n- **Precise facts in the log** — \"add label bug\" is a better historical record than \"set labels to [feature, bug]\".\n- **Trivial OR-Set projection** — maintain a set of `(value, add-timestamp)` pairs; a `REMOVE` with a later timestamp tombstones the entry.\n\n## Prerequisites\n\n- **xpo-3a219e** — Deterministic sort-key replay (without this, \"last writer\" is JSONL line order, which git merge can scramble)\n- **xpo-c762f7** — `merge=union` in `.gitattributes` (both sides' events must survive the merge)\n\n## Scope Boundary\n\n- No collaborative-text CRDT (Yjs/Automerge) for `description` — concurrent prose edits are rare and low-stakes\n- No vector clocks or Lamport clocks — wall-clock timestamp + actor + event-id sort key is sufficient for this use case\n- No wire protocol or sync server — git remains the transport","labels":["epic"],"status":"BACKLOG","title":"Per-field CRDT merge semantics"},"created_at":"2026-06-09T06:12:02.043418105Z","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg <nicbet@gmail.com>","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.169 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
