{"id":"xpo-16e586","type":"CREATE","payload":{"dependencies":[{"kind":"depends_on","source_id":"xpo-16e586","target_id":"xpo-3a219e"}],"description":"## Problem\n\nLabels are stored as a full `[]string` replacement in `UpdatePayload`. If Alice adds \"bug\" (setting labels to `[\"feature\", \"bug\"]`) and Bob adds \"urgent\" (setting labels to `[\"feature\", \"urgent\"]`) concurrently, after merge one addition is silently lost — the last-writer's full array wins.\n\n## Solution\n\nIntroduce two new event types:\n\n- **`LABEL_ADD`** — payload: `{ label: string }`\n- **`LABEL_REMOVE`** — payload: `{ label: string }`\n\n### Projection semantics (OR-Set)\n\nFor each issue, maintain a map of `label → { added_at, removed_at }`. During replay (events sorted by deterministic sort key):\n\n- `LABEL_ADD` sets `added_at = event.sort_key` if later than current `added_at`\n- `LABEL_REMOVE` sets `removed_at = event.sort_key` if later than current `removed_at`\n- A label is **present** when `added_at \u003e removed_at` (or `removed_at` is zero)\n\nThis means concurrent \"add bug\" and \"add urgent\" both survive. A remove only wins against adds that happened before it.\n\n### Backwards compatibility\n\nOld `UPDATE` events with a `labels` field continue to work: the projection treats them as a \"set to exactly this list\" — implicitly adding labels that appear and removing labels that don't, all at the event's sort key. New `LABEL_ADD`/`LABEL_REMOVE` events with later timestamps layer on top correctly.\n\n### CLI / MCP surface\n\n- `beats update --add-label bug` → emits `LABEL_ADD`\n- `beats update --remove-label bug` → emits `LABEL_REMOVE`\n- `beats update --labels \"bug,urgent\"` (full replacement) → still emits an `UPDATE` event for backwards compat, or emits the equivalent add/remove deltas (design choice)\n- MCP tools: add `add_labels` / `remove_labels` parameters to `beats_update`\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Define `LABEL_ADD` and `LABEL_REMOVE` event types and payloads in `internal/model/types.go`\n- [ ] Update projection in `internal/beats/projection.go` to handle OR-Set semantics for labels\n- [ ] Old `UPDATE` events with `labels` field still project correctly\n- [ ] CLI flags `--add-label` and `--remove-label`\n- [ ] MCP tool parameters `add_labels` / `remove_labels`\n- [ ] Test: concurrent add of different labels both survive after merge\n- [ ] Test: remove-before-add (add wins); add-before-remove (remove wins)","estimate":5,"labels":["feature"],"parent_id":"xpo-f23cf5","status":"BACKLOG","title":"Labels OR-Set: LABEL_ADD / LABEL_REMOVE event types"},"created_at":"2026-06-09T06:12:21.974171215Z","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg <nicbet@gmail.com>","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.169 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"xpo-f847da","type":"CREATE","payload":{"dependencies":[{"kind":"depends_on","source_id":"xpo-f847da","target_id":"xpo-3a219e"}],"description":"## Problem\n\nDependencies/links suffer the same full-replacement problem as labels. `UpdatePayload.Dependencies` is `[]Dependency` — a full overwrite. Concurrent \"add blocks B\" and \"add relates_to C\" will clobber one.\n\n## Solution\n\nIntroduce two new event types:\n\n- **`LINK_ADD`** — payload: `{ target_id: string, kind: string }` (kind = blocks, depends_on, relates_to, etc.)\n- **`LINK_REMOVE`** — payload: `{ target_id: string, kind: string }`\n\n### Projection semantics (OR-Set)\n\nSame pattern as labels: maintain a map of `(target_id, kind) → { added_at, removed_at }`. A link is present when `added_at \u003e removed_at`.\n\n### Backwards compatibility\n\nOld `UPDATE` events with a `dependencies` field treated as full replacement at the event's sort key. Old `CREATE` events with initial dependencies treated as `LINK_ADD` at creation time. New `LINK_ADD`/`LINK_REMOVE` events layer on top.\n\n### Existing `beats_link` tool\n\n`beats_link` already exists as a dedicated MCP tool. Internally it should now emit a `LINK_ADD` event instead of an `UPDATE` with the full dependency list. Similarly, a future `beats_unlink` tool emits `LINK_REMOVE`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Define `LINK_ADD` and `LINK_REMOVE` event types and payloads in `internal/model/types.go`\n- [ ] Update projection to handle OR-Set semantics for links\n- [ ] Old `UPDATE` events with `dependencies` and old `CREATE` events with initial deps still project correctly\n- [ ] `beats_link` MCP tool emits `LINK_ADD` event\n- [ ] New `beats_unlink` MCP tool / CLI command emits `LINK_REMOVE`\n- [ ] Test: concurrent link additions both survive\n- [ ] Test: unlink then re-link semantics","estimate":5,"labels":["feature"],"parent_id":"xpo-f23cf5","status":"BACKLOG","title":"Links OR-Set: LINK_ADD / LINK_REMOVE event types"},"created_at":"2026-06-09T06:12:31.456247219Z","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg <nicbet@gmail.com>","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.169 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"xpo-267e29","type":"CREATE","payload":{"dependencies":[{"kind":"depends_on","source_id":"xpo-267e29","target_id":"xpo-3a219e"}],"description":"## Problem\n\nConcurrent status transitions (e.g., one actor sets `DONE`, another sets `BLOCKED`) currently resolve by file order. After xpo-3a219e lands, they'll resolve by sort key — but the projection should also enforce the state machine so invalid transitions from stale actors don't corrupt state.\n\n## Solution\n\nDuring projection, when two `UPDATE` events set `status` with the same issue:\n\n1. Sort by deterministic sort key (timestamp, actor, event_id) — already guaranteed by xpo-3a219e.\n2. Apply in order. The last valid transition wins.\n3. **Terminal state guard**: if an issue reaches `DONE`, a concurrent in-flight `UPDATE` to `DOING` from a stale actor (earlier sort key) has already been applied and overwritten. If it has a *later* sort key, it reopens the issue — which is correct (the later actor had more recent intent).\n4. The \"losing\" status transition is still visible in `beats_history` for auditability.\n\n### Concurrent DONE vs BLOCKED\n\nBoth are valid end-states for different reasons. Sort-key ordering applies: the later one wins. The earlier transition is preserved in the event log and visible via `beats_history`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Projection applies status updates in sort-key order (not file order)\n- [ ] State machine validation: invalid transitions (e.g., `DONE` → `BACKLOG`) are skipped with a warning in history\n- [ ] `beats_history` shows superseded status transitions\n- [ ] Test: concurrent DONE vs BLOCKED resolves by sort key\n- [ ] Test: stale actor's transition applied then overwritten by later actor","estimate":3,"labels":["feature"],"parent_id":"xpo-f23cf5","status":"BACKLOG","title":"Status LWW with state-machine guard and sort-key tiebreak"},"created_at":"2026-06-09T06:12:43.340818565Z","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg <nicbet@gmail.com>","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.169 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"xpo-4fa8dd","type":"CREATE","payload":{"dependencies":[{"kind":"depends_on","source_id":"xpo-4fa8dd","target_id":"xpo-3a219e"}],"description":"## Problem\n\nConcurrent description edits are rare and low-stakes, but the losing edit currently vanishes silently. The winner should stick (LWW by sort key), but the loser should be visible in the audit trail.\n\n## Solution\n\nNo change to the projection logic — description remains LWW by sort key (once xpo-3a219e lands). The change is in **presentation**:\n\n- `beats_history` should show when a description update was superseded by a concurrent edit with a later sort key.\n- The superseded description text should be viewable (e.g., `beats history \u003cid\u003e` shows the full text of the losing edit).\n\n**Explicitly out of scope**: collaborative-text CRDT (Yjs/Automerge). The machinery would dwarf the rest of the tool for negligible benefit.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Description projection uses sort-key ordering (free once xpo-3a219e lands)\n- [ ] `beats_history` flags superseded description edits\n- [ ] Superseded description text is viewable in history output\n- [ ] Test: concurrent description edits, later sort key wins, earlier visible in history","estimate":2,"labels":["feature"],"parent_id":"xpo-f23cf5","status":"BACKLOG","title":"Description LWW with loser preserved in history"},"created_at":"2026-06-09T06:12:50.897599769Z","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg <nicbet@gmail.com>","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.169 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"xpo-730ec2","type":"CREATE","payload":{"dependencies":[{"kind":"depends_on","source_id":"xpo-730ec2","target_id":"xpo-3a219e"}],"description":"## Problem\n\n`sort_order` already uses fractional indexing via `sortorder.GenerateNKeysBetween`. But if two actors concurrently insert an item between the same two neighbors, they'll generate the same fractional key. There's no tiebreak — the items end up in arbitrary order.\n\n## Solution\n\nWhen two `sort_order` values are identical, tiebreak by `actor_id` (lexicographic). This is deterministic and consistent across clones.\n\nThe probability of collision is low (fractional indexing has a large key space), but when it happens, the tiebreak ensures convergence rather than non-determinism.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Projection tiebreaks identical `sort_order` values by actor ID\n- [ ] Test: two events set same `sort_order`, deterministic ordering by actor\n- [ ] Verify existing fractional indexing logic handles the edge case","estimate":1,"labels":["feature"],"parent_id":"xpo-f23cf5","status":"BACKLOG","title":"Sort-order fractional indexing: actor tiebreak"},"created_at":"2026-06-09T06:12:56.921608897Z","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg <nicbet@gmail.com>","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.169 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
