{"id":"xpo-4889dc","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"Demo project generator (`xpo demo`)","description":"## Summary\n\nAdd an `xpo demo` command that generates a realistic, fully-populated `issues.db` for a fictional project — **PurrEats**, a food delivery app for cats. The generated board should showcase every xpo feature and give new users something rich to explore immediately.\n\n## Approach: hybrid template + runtime timestamps\n\nPre-author all narrative content (titles, descriptions, comments, personas, story arcs) with hand-crafted LLM quality, but store every timestamp as a **relative offset** from \"now\" (e.g. `T-45d`, `T-12h 30m`). When `xpo demo` runs it anchors to the current time and resolves all offsets to real dates, so the dashboard's \"last 14 days\" view lights up on day one.\n\nThe seed data lives as a structured template file bundled with the binary. The generator walks events in chronological order, stamps them, and writes the final `issues.db`.\n\n## Cast (~12 personas)\n\nMix of human devs, designers, PMs, QA, and AI agents with distinct names and email addresses. Each persona should have a recognizable voice in their comments.\n\n## Scale \u0026 shape\n\n- **200–400 issues** across epics, features, bugs, and tasks\n- Realistic distribution: mostly DONE, a healthy chunk in-flight (DOING/PLANNED), some BACKLOG, a few BLOCKED\n- **Story points**, **labels**, **parent/child** relationships, **dependency links**\n- **Comments** on most issues — status updates, code review notes, design discussions, incident postmortems\n- **Realistic cycle times** — some tickets closed same-day, others dragging for weeks\n\n## Narrative arcs\n\nThe project history should tell a story with recognizable patterns:\n\n1. **Project kickoff** — epics carved, initial backlog groomed\n2. **First sprint push** — steady velocity, optimistic estimates\n3. **Production incident** — a payments bug causes a fire drill, spawns urgent hotfix tickets and a postmortem\n4. **Feature descope** — a planned feature gets cut due to timeline pressure, tickets moved back to backlog\n5. **Refactor epic** — tech debt cleanup after the incident\n6. **Launch crunch** — final push with increased throughput and terse commit-style comments\n7. **Post-launch** — bug reports trickling in, velocity normalizing\n\n## Deliverables\n\n- [ ] Seed data template file with relative timestamps\n- [ ] `xpo demo` CLI command that generates `issues.db` from the template\n- [ ] The generated DB exercises: issues, comments, status transitions, links, epics/sub-issues, story points, labels, assignees, and realistic audit history","status":"BACKLOG","labels":["feature"]},"created_at":"2026-06-17T13:24:46.114307561Z","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg <nicbet@gmail.com>","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.179 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"xpo-4889dc","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"description":"## Summary\n\nAdd an `xpo demo` command that generates a realistic, fully-populated `issues.db` for a fictional project — **PurrEats**, a food delivery app for cats (think Uber Eats, but for cats). The generated board should showcase every xpo feature and give new users something rich to explore immediately.\n\n## Approach: hybrid template + runtime timestamps\n\nPre-author all narrative content (titles, descriptions, comments, personas, story arcs) with hand-crafted LLM quality, but store every timestamp as a **relative offset** from \"now\" (e.g. `T-45d`, `T-12h 30m`). When `xpo demo` runs it anchors to the current time and resolves all offsets to real dates, so the dashboard's \"last 14 days\" view lights up on day one.\n\nThe seed data lives as a structured template file bundled with the binary. The generator walks events in chronological order, stamps them, and writes the final `issues.db`.\n\n## Cast (~12 personas)\n\nMix of human devs, designers, PMs, QA, and AI agents with distinct names and email addresses. Each persona should have a recognizable voice in their comments.\n\n## Scale \u0026 shape\n\n- **200–400 issues** across epics, features, bugs, and tasks\n- Realistic distribution: mostly DONE, a healthy chunk in-flight (DOING/PLANNED), some BACKLOG, a few BLOCKED\n- **Story points**, **labels**, **parent/child** relationships, **dependency links**\n- **Comments** on most issues — status updates, code review notes, design discussions, incident postmortems\n- **Realistic cycle times** — some tickets closed same-day, others dragging for weeks\n\n## Narrative arcs\n\nThe project history should tell a story with recognizable patterns:\n\n1. **Project kickoff** — epics carved, initial backlog groomed\n2. **First sprint push** — steady velocity, optimistic estimates\n3. **Production incident** — a payments bug causes a fire drill, spawns urgent hotfix tickets and a postmortem\n4. **Feature descope** — a planned feature gets cut due to timeline pressure, tickets moved back to backlog\n5. **Refactor epic** — tech debt cleanup after the incident\n6. **Launch crunch** — final push with increased throughput and terse commit-style comments\n7. **Post-launch** — bug reports trickling in, velocity normalizing\n\n## Deliverables\n\n- [ ] Seed data template file with relative timestamps\n- [ ] `xpo demo` CLI command that generates `issues.db` from the template\n- [ ] The generated DB exercises: issues, comments, status transitions, links, epics/sub-issues, story points, labels, assignees, and realistic audit history"},"created_at":"2026-06-17T13:25:38.157290106Z","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg <nicbet@gmail.com>","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.179 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"xpo-4889dc","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"title":"Demo project generator (`xpo demo`)","description":"## Summary\n\nAdd an `xpo demo` command that generates a realistic, fully-populated `issues.db` for a fictional project — **Upurr Eats**, a food delivery app for cats (think Uber Eats, but for cats). The generated board should showcase every xpo feature and give new users something rich to explore immediately.\n\n## Approach: hybrid template + runtime timestamps\n\nPre-author all narrative content (titles, descriptions, comments, personas, story arcs) with hand-crafted LLM quality, but store every timestamp as a **relative offset** from \"now\" (e.g. `T-45d`, `T-12h 30m`). When `xpo demo` runs it anchors to the current time and resolves all offsets to real dates, so the dashboard's \"last 14 days\" view lights up on day one.\n\nThe seed data lives as a structured template file bundled with the binary. The generator walks events in chronological order, stamps them, and writes the final `issues.db`.\n\n## Cast (~12 personas)\n\nMix of human devs, designers, PMs, QA, and AI agents with distinct names and email addresses. Each persona should have a recognizable voice in their comments.\n\n## Scale \u0026 shape\n\n- **200–400 issues** across epics, features, bugs, and tasks\n- Realistic distribution: mostly DONE, a healthy chunk in-flight (DOING/PLANNED), some BACKLOG, a few BLOCKED\n- **Story points**, **labels**, **parent/child** relationships, **dependency links**\n- **Comments** on most issues — status updates, code review notes, design discussions, incident postmortems\n- **Realistic cycle times** — some tickets closed same-day, others dragging for weeks\n\n## Narrative arcs\n\nThe project history should tell a story with recognizable patterns:\n\n1. **Project kickoff** — epics carved, initial backlog groomed\n2. **First sprint push** — steady velocity, optimistic estimates\n3. **Production incident** — a payments bug causes a fire drill, spawns urgent hotfix tickets and a postmortem\n4. **Feature descope** — a planned feature gets cut due to timeline pressure, tickets moved back to backlog\n5. **Refactor epic** — tech debt cleanup after the incident\n6. **Launch crunch** — final push with increased throughput and terse commit-style comments\n7. **Post-launch** — bug reports trickling in, velocity normalizing\n\n## Deliverables\n\n- [ ] Seed data template file with relative timestamps\n- [ ] `xpo demo` CLI command that generates `issues.db` from the template\n- [ ] The generated DB exercises: issues, comments, status transitions, links, epics/sub-issues, story points, labels, assignees, and realistic audit history"},"created_at":"2026-06-17T13:26:10.026605408Z","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg <nicbet@gmail.com>","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.179 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"xpo-4889dc","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"description":"## Summary\n\nAdd an `xpo demo` command that generates a realistic, fully-populated `issues.db` for a fictional project — **Upurr Eats**, a food delivery app for cats (think Uber Eats, but for cats). The generated board should showcase every xpo feature and give new users something rich to explore immediately.\n\n## Safety\n\n`xpo demo` must **never overwrite an existing `issues.db`**. The command should:\n\n- Require an explicit output path (e.g. `xpo demo ./upurr-eats/`)\n- Refuse to run if the target directory already contains an `issues.db`, unless `--force` is passed\n- Never write to the current project's tracker database by default\n\n## Approach: hybrid template + runtime timestamps\n\nPre-author all narrative content (titles, descriptions, comments, personas, story arcs) with hand-crafted LLM quality, but store every timestamp as a **relative offset** from \"now\" (e.g. `T-45d`, `T-12h 30m`). When `xpo demo` runs it anchors to the current time and resolves all offsets to real dates, so the dashboard's \"last 14 days\" view lights up on day one.\n\nThe seed data lives as a structured template file bundled with the binary. The generator walks events in chronological order, stamps them, and writes the final `issues.db`.\n\n## Cast (~12 personas)\n\nMix of human devs, designers, PMs, QA, and AI agents with distinct names and email addresses. Each persona should have a recognizable voice in their comments.\n\n## Scale \u0026 shape\n\n- **200–400 issues** across epics, features, bugs, and tasks\n- Realistic distribution: mostly DONE, a healthy chunk in-flight (DOING/PLANNED), some BACKLOG, a few BLOCKED\n- **Story points**, **labels**, **parent/child** relationships, **dependency links**\n- **Comments** on most issues — status updates, code review notes, design discussions, incident postmortems\n- **Realistic cycle times** — some tickets closed same-day, others dragging for weeks\n\n## Narrative arcs\n\nThe project history should tell a story with recognizable patterns:\n\n1. **Project kickoff** — epics carved, initial backlog groomed\n2. **First sprint push** — steady velocity, optimistic estimates\n3. **Production incident** — a payments bug causes a fire drill, spawns urgent hotfix tickets and a postmortem\n4. **Feature descope** — a planned feature gets cut due to timeline pressure, tickets moved back to backlog\n5. **Refactor epic** — tech debt cleanup after the incident\n6. **Launch crunch** — final push with increased throughput and terse commit-style comments\n7. **Post-launch** — bug reports trickling in, velocity normalizing\n\n## Deliverables\n\n- [ ] Seed data template file with relative timestamps\n- [ ] `xpo demo` CLI command that generates `issues.db` from the template\n- [ ] Safety: require explicit output path, refuse to overwrite existing `issues.db` without `--force`\n- [ ] The generated DB exercises: issues, comments, status transitions, links, epics/sub-issues, story points, labels, assignees, and realistic audit history"},"created_at":"2026-06-17T13:28:01.029940576Z","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg <nicbet@gmail.com>","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.179 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
