{"id":"xpo-3572a8","type":"CREATE","payload":{"dependencies":[{"kind":"depends_on","source_id":"xpo-3572a8","target_id":"xpo-e282c2"}],"description":"Implement `xpo loop`, the continuous counterpart to `xpo drive`. While `xpo drive` handles a single ticket per invocation, `xpo loop` wraps it in the \"agentic loop\" pattern — continuously picking up and completing tickets until the backlog is drained or a budget is exhausted.\n\nThis follows the looping paradigm gaining traction in the AI agent ecosystem: instead of one-shot task execution, the agent runs in a sustained loop with backoff, observability, and guardrails built in.\n\n## Behavior\n\n```\nxpo loop --agent=claude --budget=20\n● Loop started (budget: 20 tickets, backoff: exponential)\n  ✔ [xpo-4fa8dd] Description LWW — DONE (2 pts, 12m)\n  ✔ [xpo-267e29] Status guard — DONE (3 pts, 18m)\n  ✘ [xpo-8bc123] Auth refactor — BLOCKED (moved to blocked)\n  ✔ [xpo-9de456] Fix sort order — DONE (1 pt, 4m)\n  …\n● Loop paused: 3 consecutive failures, backing off 5m\n● Loop resumed\n  …\n● Loop complete: 14/20 done, 4 blocked, 2 skipped\n```\n\n## Execution model\n\n- Each iteration calls the equivalent of `xpo drive` internally\n- On success: immediately pick the next ticket (no delay)\n- On failure/block: exponential backoff (30s → 1m → 2m → 5m cap) to avoid hammering a broken area\n- On empty backlog: exit cleanly\n\n## Guardrails\n\n- `--budget=N` — max tickets to attempt (required, no unbounded loops)\n- `--max-failures=N` — consecutive failure limit before stopping (default: 5)\n- `--cooldown=DURATION` — minimum pause between tickets (default: 0)\n- `--filter=LABEL` — scope to specific labels/epics\n- `--dry-run` — show the planned execution order without acting\n\n## Observability\n\n- Live progress output with running totals (done/blocked/skipped)\n- Summary report at exit with aggregate stats (points completed, total cycle time, failure rate)\n- Each ticket's comment trail is written by `xpo drive` as usual\n\n## Relationship to `xpo drive`\n\n`xpo loop` composes `xpo drive` — it does not duplicate its logic. `xpo drive` remains the single-ticket primitive; `xpo loop` is the orchestrator.","labels":["feature","CLI"],"title":"\"`xpo loop` — continuous agent execution with backoff and budget\""},"created_at":"2026-06-18T09:47:57.475062Z","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg <nicbet@gmail.com>","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.179 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"xpo-3572a8","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"sort_order":"a3x","status":"PLANNED","title":"`xpo loop` — continuous agent execution with backoff and budget"},"created_at":"2026-06-18T09:48:53.223811Z","created_by":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e"}
{"id":"xpo-643465","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"Relocate the project selector from its current position in the sidebar to the bottom of the left nav. Convert it from a static display into a dropdown/select control.\n\n## Rationale\n\nThe project context is important but not something you switch frequently — it belongs in a fixed footer position rather than taking up prime navigation space. Matches the pattern used by similar tools.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Dropdown at the bottom of the sidebar showing the current project name and port\n- Clicking opens a select menu listing available projects\n- Selecting a project switches context (reloads issues for the chosen project)\n- Current project is visually highlighted in the dropdown","labels":["feature","WebApp"],"title":"Move project selector to bottom of sidebar as a dropdown"},"created_at":"2026-06-18T09:48:48.777229Z","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg <nicbet@gmail.com>","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.179 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"xpo-643465","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"sort_order":"a0V","status":"PLANNED"},"created_at":"2026-06-18T09:53:55.431512Z","created_by":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e"}
{"id":"xpo-8d8169","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"Unestimated issues currently show no estimate indicator. Since they're counted as 1 point everywhere (velocity, cycle stats, etc.), they should render with:\n\n- A **dashed triangle icon** — visually distinct from the solid triangle used for deliberately estimated issues, signaling \"default, not deliberate\"\n- The value **1** next to it — showing what's actually being counted\n\nThis makes the implicit default visible without hiding information.","labels":["improvement","WebApp"],"title":"Show dashed triangle icon with default \"1\" for unestimated issues"},"created_at":"2026-06-18T09:50:32.982742Z","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg <nicbet@gmail.com>","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.179 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"xpo-8d8169","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"sort_order":"a0","status":"PLANNED"},"created_at":"2026-06-18T09:51:02.963169Z","created_by":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e"}
{"id":"xpo-b8f7e8","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"Issue creates/updates from CLI and MCP tools take up to 30 seconds to appear in the GUI. Changes made through the web UI appear instantly because the HTTP handlers call `broadcastEvent()` directly, but CLI/MCP writes go straight to `issues.db`, bypassing SSE entirely. The GUI relies on a 30-second fallback polling interval to pick these up.\n\n## Root cause\n\n- `internal/server/handlers.go` calls `s.broadcastEvent()` after each mutation — instant for web UI changes\n- CLI and MCP tools write to `issues.db` directly, never hitting the HTTP handlers\n- `web/src/hooks/useSSE.ts` runs a 30s fallback poll to catch these external changes\n\n## Fix\n\nAdd filesystem watching (fsnotify) on `issues.db` in the Go server. When the file is modified externally, broadcast a generic SSE event so the GUI refreshes immediately. Debounce briefly (100-200ms) to coalesce rapid successive writes.","labels":["bug","CLI"],"status":"PLANNED","title":"Watch issues.db for external changes and broadcast SSE"},"created_at":"2026-06-18T09:53:40.96848Z","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg <nicbet@gmail.com>","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.179 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
