{"id":"xpo-a18d46","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"sort_order":"ZvV"},"created_at":"2026-08-31T11:25:42.074306Z","created_by":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"web"}
{"id":"xpo-3798b7","type":"CREATE","payload":{"dependencies":[{"kind":"relates_to","source_id":"xpo-3798b7","target_id":"xpo-748873"},{"kind":"relates_to","source_id":"xpo-3798b7","target_id":"xpo-6598d3"},{"kind":"relates_to","source_id":"xpo-3798b7","target_id":"xpo-3d7608"}],"description":"Epic for transforming the xpo web UI from a passive board into an active software factory — where agents are first-class participants you interact with through the browser.\n\n### Vision\n\nInvert the current \"CLI drives agent, web shows status\" model. The web UI becomes the primary surface for orchestrating AI-powered development: chat with an agent to manage issues, kick off implementation with interactive feedback loops, and review changes before merging — all without leaving the browser.\n\nThis is a strategic shift from \"CLI first\" to \"web-native with CLI escape hatch\". The CLI remains for power users and automation, but the web UI becomes the default way to work with xpo.\n\n### Pillars\n\n**1. Agent Chat Interface**\nA conversational sidebar or panel where users can interact with an AI agent to:\n- Create and edit issues (\"add a bug about the login timeout\")\n- Groom the backlog (\"what's blocking the auth epic?\", \"estimate these stories\")\n- Ask questions about existing issues, history, and dependencies\n- Trigger actions (status transitions, assignments, linking)\n\n**2. \"Start Work\" → Drive Flow**\nTransform the current \"Start Work\" button (creates worktree + transitions to DOING) into a full `xpo drive` execution:\n- Kicks off an agent that writes the spec, implements, and tests\n- Interactive feedback loop: agent surfaces open questions → user comments → agent continues or marks BLOCKED\n- Live progress view (replaces or extends the current spectator mode concept from xpo-748873)\n- User can steer, pause, or abort the drive from the UI\n\n**3. \"Merge Branch\" → Review Flow**\nTransform merge into a review-first flow:\n- Agent generates a review summary (diff walkthrough, risk assessment)\n- Interactive review: user leaves comments → agent addresses them → re-review\n- Approval gate before merge executes\n- Connects to the Code Reviews epic (xpo-6598d3) for the reviewer agent\n\n### Open Questions\n\n- Agent backend: run agents as subprocesses on the server? Containerized? Cloud-dispatched?\n- Auth model: who can trigger drives? Multi-tenant considerations?\n- Cost visibility: token/cost tracking surfaced in the UI before and during execution\n- Streaming: WebSocket or SSE for live agent output?\n- State management: how does the chat context persist across page reloads?\n- Relationship to `xpo drive` CLI: shared executor code, or web-native implementation?\n\n### Not in scope (yet)\n\n- Multi-agent collaboration in the same session\n- Voice interface\n- GitHub/GitLab integration (PR creation, CI status)\n\nThis epic needs further design work before implementation begins. Filing to capture the vision and start breaking it into plannable sub-issues.","labels":["epic","feature"],"sort_order":"bPR","status":"BACKLOG","title":"AI-native Web UI: agent chat, drive, and review flows"},"created_at":"2026-08-31T11:33:37.561423Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.251 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"xpo-3798b7","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"description":"Epic for transforming the xpo web UI from a passive board into an active software factory — where agents are first-class participants you interact with through the browser.\n\n## Vision\n\nInvert the current \"CLI drives agent, web shows status\" model. The web UI becomes the primary surface for orchestrating AI-powered development: chat with an agent to manage issues, kick off implementation with interactive feedback loops, and review changes before merging — all without leaving the browser.\n\nThe CLI remains for power users and automation. `xpo drive` is the interactive CLI equivalent of the web UI's \"Start Work\" flow. `xpo deliver` is the non-interactive fire-and-forget variant where agents make all decisions autonomously.\n\n## Architecture\n\n### One engine, two surfaces\n\n`DriveIssue()` is the backend engine regardless of who starts it. Trail recording happens at this layer — trails always land on disk as artifacts. The web UI starts a drive via the API and subscribes to live updates via SSE (a real-time projection of the trail file as it's written). Interactive feedback goes through the existing comment/update API.\n\n### Agent hierarchy\n\nFour agents with clear separation, each with dedicated system prompts and potentially different model configurations:\n\n- **Manager** — owns the state machine (spec → implement → review → tophat → merge), invokes sub-agents, handles phase transitions and trail recording. No opinions on code or product — pure orchestration.\n- **Coder** — implementation specialist. Writes code, tests, fixes. Focused prompt with spec and test commands. Could run on a fast/cheap model for iteration speed.\n- **Reviewer** — adversarial critic. Reviews diffs for correctness, simplification, edge cases. Fresh context (no sunk-cost bias from writing the code). Could run on a strong reasoning model. Invoked twice: once as a code quality gate before completion comment, and optionally to pre-screen the diff during user tophat.\n- **Decider** — product owner proxy (non-interactive only). Answers open questions with reasonable defaults, documents rationale. Instructions biased toward \"pick the simpler option and flag it for revisit.\"\n\n### Interactive flow (Web UI / `xpo drive`)\n\n1. Start Work creates a worktree, transitions issue to DOING\n2. Agent investigates whether a spec artifact exists or needs creation\n3. If spec has open questions: prompt (CLI) or comment + transition to BLOCKED (Web)\n4. User responds (types in CLI / posts comment reply on Web)\n5. Repeat until spec is satisfied, then proceed to implementation\n6. Implementation runs in background, trail events stream to user via SSE\n7. Adversarial reviewer critiques the coder's work, possibly sending it back for another round\n8. Completion comment surfaces when implementation passes review\n9. User tophats the worktree, inspects diff\n10. If user has review comments: type in CLI / add comment on issue\n11. Coder updates spec if necessary, does another code loop, posts revision comment\n12. Repeat until user approves → walkthrough artifact written\n13. Merge closes issue, records final trail metrics, merges worktree into main\n\n### Non-interactive flow (`xpo deliver`)\n\nSame phases as interactive, but at every point where the flow would block waiting for user input, the Decider agent is invoked to provide an answer. The Decider documents its choices in the spec under \"Agent Decisions\" (question, choice, reasoning). The adversarial Reviewer is the quality gate that compensates for the user not being in the loop.\n\n## Pillars\n\n### 1. Agent Chat Interface\nConversational sidebar/panel for interacting with an AI agent to:\n- Create and edit issues (\"add a bug about the login timeout\")\n- Groom the backlog (\"what's blocking the auth epic?\", \"estimate these stories\")\n- Ask questions about existing issues, history, and dependencies\n- Trigger actions (status transitions, assignments, linking)\n\n### 2. \"Start Work\" → Drive Flow\nTransform the current button into a full drive execution with live streaming and interactive feedback. The existing \"needs attention\" dashboard badge handles notification when issues are BLOCKED waiting for user input.\n\n### 3. \"Merge Branch\" → Review Flow\nTransform merge into a review-first flow: reviewer agent generates a review summary, user leaves comments, coder addresses them, repeat until approved. Connects to the Code Reviews epic (xpo-6598d3).\n\n### 4. Backend infrastructure\n- API endpoint to start/stop a drive (spawns `DriveIssue()` server-side, returns session ID)\n- SSE endpoint that tails the trail file (streams trail events as they're written)\n- Trail recording (xpo-748873) is a prerequisite — it's the data format the SSE stream serves\n- `xpo deliver` CLI command for non-interactive mode\n\n## Related epics\n\n- xpo-748873 — Drive trails (prerequisite: trail format is the SSE data source)\n- xpo-6598d3 — Code Reviews (reviewer agent, review flow)\n- xpo-3d7608 — Drive \u0026 Loop robustness (auto-recovery, sandboxing)\n- xpo-f12313 — Multi-agent executors (sub-agent model configs)\n\n## Open Questions\n\n- Agent backend: subprocess on the server? Containerized? Resource limits?\n- Auth model: who can trigger drives? Cost implications for multi-tenant?\n- Cost visibility: token/cost tracking surfaced in the UI before and during execution\n- State management: how does chat context persist across page reloads?\n- `xpo loop` stays CLI-only for now — not part of web UI scope\n\n## Not in scope (yet)\n\n- Multi-agent collaboration in the same session\n- Voice interface\n- GitHub/GitLab integration (PR creation, CI status)\n\nThis epic needs further design work before implementation begins. Filing to capture the vision and architectural decisions."},"created_at":"2026-08-31T11:58:36.680903Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.251 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"xpo-3798b7","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"description":"Epic for transforming the xpo web UI from a passive board into an active software factory — where agents are first-class participants you interact with through the browser.\n\n## Vision\n\nInvert the current \"CLI drives agent, web shows status\" model. The web UI becomes the primary surface for orchestrating AI-powered development: chat with an agent to manage issues, kick off implementation with interactive feedback loops, and review changes before merging — all without leaving the browser.\n\nThe CLI remains for power users and automation. `xpo drive` is the interactive CLI equivalent of the web UI's \"Start Work\" flow. `xpo deliver` is the non-interactive fire-and-forget variant where agents make all decisions autonomously.\n\n## Architecture\n\n### One engine, two surfaces\n\n`DriveIssue()` is the backend engine regardless of who starts it. Trail recording happens at this layer — trails always land on disk as artifacts. The web UI starts a drive via the API and subscribes to live updates via SSE (a real-time projection of the trail file as it's written). Interactive feedback goes through the existing comment/update API.\n\n### Process model\n\nDetached subprocess with PID tracking. Agents run in their own process group, surviving server restarts. State is durable via trail files on disk:\n\n- Spawn agent in its own process group\n- Write PID to `.xpo/drives/\u003cissue-id\u003e.pid` alongside the trail file\n- On server restart: scan for active PIDs, reconnect SSE streams to their trail files\n- On agent death: PID check fails, mark drive as interrupted\n- Multiple concurrent drives supported — each is an independent process with its own trail\n\nNo containers, no queue system, no new dependencies. Resource limits (ulimit/cgroups on the child process) can be added later without architectural changes.\n\n### Agent hierarchy\n\nFour agents with clear separation, each with dedicated system prompts and potentially different model configurations:\n\n- **Manager** — owns the state machine (spec → implement → review → tophat → merge), invokes sub-agents, handles phase transitions and trail recording. No opinions on code or product — pure orchestration.\n- **Coder** — implementation specialist. Writes code, tests, fixes. Focused prompt with spec and test commands. Could run on a fast/cheap model for iteration speed.\n- **Reviewer** — adversarial critic. Reviews diffs for correctness, simplification, edge cases. Fresh context (no sunk-cost bias from writing the code). Could run on a strong reasoning model. Invoked twice: once as a code quality gate before completion comment, and optionally to pre-screen the diff during user tophat.\n- **Decider** — product owner proxy (non-interactive only). Answers open questions with reasonable defaults, documents rationale. Instructions biased toward \"pick the simpler option and flag it for revisit.\"\n\n### Interactive flow (Web UI / `xpo drive`)\n\n1. Start Work creates a worktree, transitions issue to DOING\n2. Agent investigates whether a spec artifact exists or needs creation\n3. If spec has open questions: prompt (CLI) or comment + transition to BLOCKED (Web)\n4. User responds (types in CLI / posts comment reply on Web)\n5. Repeat until spec is satisfied, then proceed to implementation\n6. Implementation runs in background, trail events stream to user via SSE\n7. Adversarial reviewer critiques the coder's work, possibly sending it back for another round\n8. Completion comment surfaces when implementation passes review\n9. User tophats the worktree, inspects diff\n10. If user has review comments: type in CLI / add comment on issue\n11. Coder updates spec if necessary, does another code loop, posts revision comment\n12. Repeat until user approves → walkthrough artifact written\n13. Merge closes issue, records final trail metrics, merges worktree into main\n\n### Non-interactive flow (`xpo deliver`)\n\nSame phases as interactive, but at every point where the flow would block waiting for user input, the Decider agent is invoked to provide an answer. The Decider documents its choices in the spec under \"Agent Decisions\" (question, choice, reasoning). The adversarial Reviewer is the quality gate that compensates for the user not being in the loop.\n\n## Pillars\n\n### 1. Agent Chat Interface\nConversational sidebar/panel for interacting with an AI agent to:\n- Create and edit issues (\"add a bug about the login timeout\")\n- Groom the backlog (\"what's blocking the auth epic?\", \"estimate these stories\")\n- Ask questions about existing issues, history, and dependencies\n- Trigger actions (status transitions, assignments, linking)\n\n### 2. \"Start Work\" → Drive Flow\nTransform the current button into a full drive execution with live streaming and interactive feedback. The existing \"needs attention\" dashboard badge handles notification when issues are BLOCKED waiting for user input.\n\n### 3. \"Merge Branch\" → Review Flow\nTransform merge into a review-first flow: reviewer agent generates a review summary, user leaves comments, coder addresses them, repeat until approved. Connects to the Code Reviews epic (xpo-6598d3).\n\n### 4. Backend infrastructure\n- API endpoint to start/stop a drive (spawns agent as detached subprocess, returns session ID)\n- SSE endpoint that tails the trail file (streams trail events as they're written)\n- PID tracking in `.xpo/drives/` for lifecycle management and server reconnection\n- Trail recording (xpo-748873) is a prerequisite — it's the data format the SSE stream serves\n- `xpo deliver` CLI command for non-interactive mode\n\n## Decisions\n\n| Question | Decision | Rationale |\n|----------|----------|-----------|\n| Agent backend | Detached subprocess with PID tracking | Simplest model that survives server restarts, supports concurrent drives, and needs no new dependencies. ~50 lines of PID lifecycle code. Containerization becomes a future sub-issue when multi-tenant or scaling demands it. |\n| `xpo loop` in web UI | CLI-only for now | Loop is a power-user automation tool; web UI focuses on the interactive drive flow |\n\n## Open Questions\n\n- Auth model: who can trigger drives? Cost implications for multi-tenant?\n- Cost visibility: token/cost tracking surfaced in the UI before and during execution\n- State management: how does chat context persist across page reloads?\n\n## Related epics\n\n- xpo-748873 — Drive trails (prerequisite: trail format is the SSE data source)\n- xpo-6598d3 — Code Reviews (reviewer agent, review flow)\n- xpo-3d7608 — Drive \u0026 Loop robustness (auto-recovery, sandboxing)\n- xpo-f12313 — Multi-agent executors (sub-agent model configs)\n\n## Not in scope (yet)\n\n- Multi-agent collaboration in the same session\n- Voice interface\n- GitHub/GitLab integration (PR creation, CI status)\n\nThis epic needs further design work before implementation begins. Filing to capture the vision and architectural decisions."},"created_at":"2026-08-31T12:08:26.078087Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.251 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"xpo-3798b7","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"description":"Epic for transforming the xpo web UI from a passive board into an active software factory — where agents are first-class participants you interact with through the browser.\n\n## Vision\n\nInvert the current \"CLI drives agent, web shows status\" model. The web UI becomes the primary surface for orchestrating AI-powered development: chat with an agent to manage issues, kick off implementation with interactive feedback loops, and review changes before merging — all without leaving the browser.\n\nThe CLI remains for power users and automation. `xpo drive` is the interactive CLI equivalent of the web UI's \"Start Work\" flow. `xpo deliver` is the non-interactive fire-and-forget variant where agents make all decisions autonomously.\n\n## Architecture\n\n### One engine, two surfaces\n\n`DriveIssue()` is the backend engine regardless of who starts it. Trail recording happens at this layer — trails always land on disk as artifacts. The web UI starts a drive via the API and subscribes to live updates via SSE (a real-time projection of the trail file as it's written). Interactive feedback goes through the existing comment/update API.\n\n### Process model\n\nDetached subprocess with PID tracking. Agents run in their own process group, surviving server restarts. State is durable via trail files on disk:\n\n- Spawn agent in its own process group\n- Write PID to `.xpo/drives/\u003cissue-id\u003e.pid` alongside the trail file\n- On server restart: scan for active PIDs, reconnect SSE streams to their trail files\n- On agent death: PID check fails, mark drive as interrupted\n- Multiple concurrent drives supported — each is an independent process with its own trail\n\nNo containers, no queue system, no new dependencies. Resource limits (ulimit/cgroups on the child process) can be added later without architectural changes.\n\n### Agent hierarchy\n\nFour agents with clear separation, each with dedicated system prompts and potentially different model configurations:\n\n- **Manager** — owns the state machine (spec → implement → review → tophat → merge), invokes sub-agents, handles phase transitions and trail recording. No opinions on code or product — pure orchestration.\n- **Coder** — implementation specialist. Writes code, tests, fixes. Focused prompt with spec and test commands. Could run on a fast/cheap model for iteration speed.\n- **Reviewer** — adversarial critic. Reviews diffs for correctness, simplification, edge cases. Fresh context (no sunk-cost bias from writing the code). Could run on a strong reasoning model. Invoked twice: once as a code quality gate before completion comment, and optionally to pre-screen the diff during user tophat.\n- **Decider** — product owner proxy (non-interactive only). Answers open questions with reasonable defaults, documents rationale. Instructions biased toward \"pick the simpler option and flag it for revisit.\"\n\n### Interactive flow (Web UI / `xpo drive`)\n\n1. Start Work creates a worktree, transitions issue to DOING\n2. Agent investigates whether a spec artifact exists or needs creation\n3. If spec has open questions: prompt (CLI) or comment + transition to BLOCKED (Web)\n4. User responds (types in CLI / posts comment reply on Web)\n5. Repeat until spec is satisfied, then proceed to implementation\n6. Implementation runs in background, trail events stream to user via SSE\n7. Adversarial reviewer critiques the coder's work, possibly sending it back for another round\n8. Completion comment surfaces when implementation passes review\n9. User tophats the worktree, inspects diff\n10. If user has review comments: type in CLI / add comment on issue\n11. Coder updates spec if necessary, does another code loop, posts revision comment\n12. Repeat until user approves → walkthrough artifact written\n13. Merge closes issue, records final trail metrics, merges worktree into main\n\n### Non-interactive flow (`xpo deliver`)\n\nSame phases as interactive, but at every point where the flow would block waiting for user input, the Decider agent is invoked to provide an answer. The Decider documents its choices in the spec under \"Agent Decisions\" (question, choice, reasoning). The adversarial Reviewer is the quality gate that compensates for the user not being in the loop.\n\n## Pillars\n\n### 1. Agent Chat Interface\nConversational sidebar/panel for interacting with an AI agent to:\n- Create and edit issues (\"add a bug about the login timeout\")\n- Groom the backlog (\"what's blocking the auth epic?\", \"estimate these stories\")\n- Ask questions about existing issues, history, and dependencies\n- Trigger actions (status transitions, assignments, linking)\n\n### 2. \"Start Work\" → Drive Flow\nTransform the current button into a full drive execution with live streaming and interactive feedback. The existing \"needs attention\" dashboard badge handles notification when issues are BLOCKED waiting for user input.\n\n### 3. \"Merge Branch\" → Review Flow\nTransform merge into a review-first flow: reviewer agent generates a review summary, user leaves comments, coder addresses them, repeat until approved. Connects to the Code Reviews epic (xpo-6598d3).\n\n### 4. Backend infrastructure\n- API endpoint to start/stop a drive (spawns agent as detached subprocess, returns session ID)\n- SSE endpoint that tails the trail file (streams trail events as they're written)\n- PID tracking in `.xpo/drives/` for lifecycle management and server reconnection\n- Trail recording (xpo-748873) is a prerequisite — it's the data format the SSE stream serves\n- `xpo deliver` CLI command for non-interactive mode\n\n## Decisions\n\n| Question | Decision | Rationale |\n|----------|----------|-----------|\n| Agent backend | Detached subprocess with PID tracking | Simplest model that survives server restarts, supports concurrent drives, and needs no new dependencies. ~50 lines of PID lifecycle code. Containerization becomes a future sub-issue when multi-tenant or scaling demands it. |\n| `xpo loop` in web UI | CLI-only for now | Loop is a power-user automation tool; web UI focuses on the interactive drive flow |\n| Auth / permissions | Authenticated = authorized | SSH key identity is sufficient. Any authenticated user can trigger drives. RBAC is a separate epic if multi-tenant scenarios materialize. |\n| Cost visibility | Surface existing AgentStats in DriveResult and trail events | Token tracking (TokensIn, TokensOut, CostUSD, Duration) already exists per-call in AgentResult and accumulated in AgentStats via the executor. Currently only logged to terminal. Fix: add AgentStats to DriveResult, include cumulative cost in trail events so the SSE stream can show live cost in the web UI. |\n\n## Open Questions\n\n- State management: how does chat context persist across page reloads?\n\n## Related epics\n\n- xpo-748873 — Drive trails (prerequisite: trail format is the SSE data source)\n- xpo-6598d3 — Code Reviews (reviewer agent, review flow)\n- xpo-3d7608 — Drive \u0026 Loop robustness (auto-recovery, sandboxing)\n- xpo-f12313 — Multi-agent executors (sub-agent model configs)\n\n## Not in scope (yet)\n\n- Multi-agent collaboration in the same session\n- Voice interface\n- GitHub/GitLab integration (PR creation, CI status)\n\nThis epic needs further design work before implementation begins. Filing to capture the vision and architectural decisions."},"created_at":"2026-08-31T12:13:11.282389Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.251 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"xpo-3798b7","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"description":"Epic for transforming the xpo web UI from a passive board into an active software factory — where agents are first-class participants you interact with through the browser.\n\n## Vision\n\nInvert the current \"CLI drives agent, web shows status\" model. The web UI becomes the primary surface for orchestrating AI-powered development: chat with an agent to manage issues, kick off implementation with interactive feedback loops, and review changes before merging — all without leaving the browser.\n\nThe CLI remains for power users and automation. `xpo drive` is the interactive CLI equivalent of the web UI's \"Start Work\" flow. `xpo deliver` is the non-interactive fire-and-forget variant where agents make all decisions autonomously.\n\n## Architecture\n\n### One engine, two surfaces\n\n`DriveIssue()` is the backend engine regardless of who starts it. Trail recording happens at this layer — trails always land on disk as artifacts. The web UI starts a drive via the API and subscribes to live updates via SSE (a real-time projection of the trail file as it's written). Interactive feedback goes through the existing comment/update API.\n\n### Process model\n\nDetached subprocess with PID tracking. Agents run in their own process group, surviving server restarts. State is durable via trail files on disk:\n\n- Spawn agent in its own process group\n- Write PID to `.xpo/drives/\u003cissue-id\u003e/run.pid` alongside the trail file\n- On server restart: scan for active PIDs, reconnect SSE streams to their trail files\n- On agent death: PID check fails, mark drive as interrupted\n- Multiple concurrent drives supported — each is an independent process with its own trail\n\nNo containers, no queue system, no new dependencies. Resource limits (ulimit/cgroups on the child process) can be added later without architectural changes.\n\n### State management\n\nTwo directories with different lifecycles:\n\n- **`.xpo/drives/\u003cissue-id\u003e/`** — drive state: PID file, trail, agent context. Tied to an issue. Trail files graduate to committed artifacts when the drive completes (durable record of what the agent did). Not gitignored.\n- **`.xpo/chats/`** — chat sessions: ephemeral, user-local, gitignored. Each session gets a UUID-named file with conversation history. No issue association — these are the general-purpose agent chat interactions (\"add a bug about X\", \"what's blocking the auth epic?\"). Pruned by age or count. Sharing chats between users is out of scope.\n\nKey distinction: drive state is project data (shared, committed). Chat state is personal scratch (local, disposable).\n\n### Agent hierarchy\n\nFour agents with clear separation, each with dedicated system prompts and potentially different model configurations:\n\n- **Manager** — owns the state machine (spec → implement → review → tophat → merge), invokes sub-agents, handles phase transitions and trail recording. No opinions on code or product — pure orchestration.\n- **Coder** — implementation specialist. Writes code, tests, fixes. Focused prompt with spec and test commands. Could run on a fast/cheap model for iteration speed.\n- **Reviewer** — adversarial critic. Reviews diffs for correctness, simplification, edge cases. Fresh context (no sunk-cost bias from writing the code). Could run on a strong reasoning model. Invoked twice: once as a code quality gate before completion comment, and optionally to pre-screen the diff during user tophat.\n- **Decider** — product owner proxy (non-interactive only). Answers open questions with reasonable defaults, documents rationale. Instructions biased toward \"pick the simpler option and flag it for revisit.\"\n\n### Interactive flow (Web UI / `xpo drive`)\n\n1. Start Work creates a worktree, transitions issue to DOING\n2. Agent investigates whether a spec artifact exists or needs creation\n3. If spec has open questions: prompt (CLI) or comment + transition to BLOCKED (Web)\n4. User responds (types in CLI / posts comment reply on Web)\n5. Repeat until spec is satisfied, then proceed to implementation\n6. Implementation runs in background, trail events stream to user via SSE\n7. Adversarial reviewer critiques the coder's work, possibly sending it back for another round\n8. Completion comment surfaces when implementation passes review\n9. User tophats the worktree, inspects diff\n10. If user has review comments: type in CLI / add comment on issue\n11. Coder updates spec if necessary, does another code loop, posts revision comment\n12. Repeat until user approves → walkthrough artifact written\n13. Merge closes issue, records final trail metrics, merges worktree into main\n\n### Non-interactive flow (`xpo deliver`)\n\nSame phases as interactive, but at every point where the flow would block waiting for user input, the Decider agent is invoked to provide an answer. The Decider documents its choices in the spec under \"Agent Decisions\" (question, choice, reasoning). The adversarial Reviewer is the quality gate that compensates for the user not being in the loop.\n\n## Pillars\n\n### 1. Agent Chat Interface\nConversational sidebar/panel for interacting with an AI agent to:\n- Create and edit issues (\"add a bug about the login timeout\")\n- Groom the backlog (\"what's blocking the auth epic?\", \"estimate these stories\")\n- Ask questions about existing issues, history, and dependencies\n- Trigger actions (status transitions, assignments, linking)\n\n### 2. \"Start Work\" → Drive Flow\nTransform the current button into a full drive execution with live streaming and interactive feedback. The existing \"needs attention\" dashboard badge handles notification when issues are BLOCKED waiting for user input.\n\n### 3. \"Merge Branch\" → Review Flow\nTransform merge into a review-first flow: reviewer agent generates a review summary, user leaves comments, coder addresses them, repeat until approved. Connects to the Code Reviews epic (xpo-6598d3).\n\n### 4. Backend infrastructure\n- API endpoint to start/stop a drive (spawns agent as detached subprocess, returns session ID)\n- SSE endpoint that tails the trail file (streams trail events as they're written)\n- PID tracking in `.xpo/drives/` for lifecycle management and server reconnection\n- Trail recording (xpo-748873) is a prerequisite — it's the data format the SSE stream serves\n- `xpo deliver` CLI command for non-interactive mode\n\n## Decisions\n\n| Question | Decision | Rationale |\n|----------|----------|-----------|\n| Agent backend | Detached subprocess with PID tracking | Simplest model that survives server restarts, supports concurrent drives, and needs no new dependencies. ~50 lines of PID lifecycle code. Containerization becomes a future sub-issue when multi-tenant or scaling demands it. |\n| `xpo loop` in web UI | CLI-only for now | Loop is a power-user automation tool; web UI focuses on the interactive drive flow |\n| Auth / permissions | Authenticated = authorized | SSH key identity is sufficient. Any authenticated user can trigger drives. RBAC is a separate epic if multi-tenant scenarios materialize. |\n| Cost visibility | Surface existing AgentStats in DriveResult and trail events | Token tracking (TokensIn, TokensOut, CostUSD, Duration) already exists per-call in AgentResult and accumulated in AgentStats via the executor. Currently only logged to terminal. Fix: add AgentStats to DriveResult, include cumulative cost in trail events so the SSE stream can show live cost in the web UI. |\n| State management | Split by purpose: `.xpo/drives/\u003cissue-id\u003e/` for drive state (committed), `.xpo/chats/` for chat sessions (gitignored, user-local) | Drive trails are project data — shared, committed as artifacts on completion. Chat sessions are personal scratch — ephemeral, no issue association, pruned by age. Different lifecycles require different storage and git treatment. |\n\n## Related epics\n\n- xpo-748873 — Drive trails (prerequisite: trail format is the SSE data source)\n- xpo-6598d3 — Code Reviews (reviewer agent, review flow)\n- xpo-3d7608 — Drive \u0026 Loop robustness (auto-recovery, sandboxing)\n- xpo-f12313 — Multi-agent executors (sub-agent model configs)\n\n## Not in scope (yet)\n\n- Multi-agent collaboration in the same session\n- Voice interface\n- GitHub/GitLab integration (PR creation, CI status)\n- Sharing chat sessions between users\n- RBAC / permission model\n\nThis epic needs further design work before implementation begins. Filing to capture the vision and architectural decisions."},"created_at":"2026-08-31T12:19:41.086795Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.251 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"xpo-3798b7","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"description":"Epic for transforming the xpo web UI from a passive board into an active software factory — where agents are first-class participants you interact with through the browser.\n\n## Vision\n\nInvert the current \"CLI drives agent, web shows status\" model. The web UI becomes the primary surface for orchestrating AI-powered development: chat with an agent to manage issues, kick off implementation with interactive feedback loops, and review changes before merging — all without leaving the browser.\n\nThe CLI remains for power users and automation. `xpo drive` is the interactive CLI equivalent of the web UI's \"Start Work\" flow. `xpo deliver` is the non-interactive fire-and-forget variant where agents make all decisions autonomously.\n\n## Architecture\n\n### One engine, two surfaces\n\n`DriveIssue()` is the backend engine regardless of who starts it. Trail recording happens at this layer — trails always land on disk as artifacts. The web UI starts a drive via the API and subscribes to live updates via SSE (a real-time projection of the trail file as it's written). Interactive feedback goes through the existing comment/update API.\n\n### Process model\n\nDetached subprocess with PID tracking. Agents run in their own process group, surviving server restarts. State is durable via trail files on disk:\n\n- Spawn agent in its own process group\n- Write PID to `.xpo/drives/\u003cissue-id\u003e/run.pid` alongside the trail file\n- On server restart: scan for active PIDs, reconnect SSE streams to their trail files\n- On agent death: PID check fails, mark drive as interrupted\n- Multiple concurrent drives supported — each is an independent process with its own trail\n\nNo containers, no queue system, no new dependencies. Resource limits (ulimit/cgroups on the child process) can be added later without architectural changes.\n\n### State management\n\nTwo directories with different lifecycles:\n\n- **`.xpo/drives/\u003cissue-id\u003e/`** — drive state: PID file, trail, agent context. Tied to an issue. Trail files graduate to committed artifacts when the drive completes (durable record of what the agent did). Not gitignored.\n- **`.xpo/chats/`** — chat sessions: ephemeral, user-local, gitignored. Each session gets a UUID-named file with conversation history. No issue association — these are the general-purpose agent chat interactions (\"add a bug about X\", \"what's blocking the auth epic?\"). Pruned by age or count. Sharing chats between users is out of scope.\n\nKey distinction: drive state is project data (shared, committed). Chat state is personal scratch (local, disposable).\n\n### Agent hierarchy\n\nFour agents with clear separation, each with dedicated system prompts and potentially different model configurations:\n\n- **Manager** — owns the state machine (spec → implement → review → tophat → merge), invokes sub-agents, handles phase transitions and trail recording. No opinions on code or product — pure orchestration.\n- **Coder** — implementation specialist. Writes code, tests, fixes. Focused prompt with spec and test commands. Could run on a fast/cheap model for iteration speed.\n- **Reviewer** — adversarial critic. Reviews diffs for correctness, simplification, edge cases. Fresh context (no sunk-cost bias from writing the code). Could run on a strong reasoning model. Invoked twice: once as a code quality gate before completion comment, and optionally to pre-screen the diff during user tophat.\n- **Decider** — product owner proxy (non-interactive only). Answers open questions with reasonable defaults, documents rationale. Instructions biased toward \"pick the simpler option and flag it for revisit.\"\n\n### Interactive flow (Web UI / `xpo drive`)\n\n1. Start Work creates a worktree, transitions issue to DOING\n2. Agent investigates whether a spec artifact exists or needs creation\n3. If spec has open questions: prompt (CLI) or comment + transition to BLOCKED (Web)\n4. User responds (types in CLI / posts comment reply on Web)\n5. Repeat until spec is satisfied, then proceed to implementation\n6. Implementation runs in background, trail events stream to user via SSE\n7. Adversarial reviewer critiques the coder's work, possibly sending it back for another round\n8. Completion comment surfaces when implementation passes review\n9. User tophats the worktree, inspects diff\n10. If user has review comments: type in CLI / add comment on issue\n11. Coder updates spec if necessary, does another code loop, posts revision comment\n12. Repeat until user approves → walkthrough artifact written\n13. Merge closes issue, records final trail metrics, merges worktree into main\n\n### Non-interactive flow (`xpo deliver`)\n\nSame phases as interactive, but at every point where the flow would block waiting for user input, the Decider agent is invoked to provide an answer. The Decider documents its choices in the spec under \"Agent Decisions\" (question, choice, reasoning). The adversarial Reviewer is the quality gate that compensates for the user not being in the loop.\n\n## Pillars\n\n### 1. Agent Chat Interface\nConversational sidebar/panel for interacting with an AI agent to:\n- Create and edit issues (\"add a bug about the login timeout\")\n- Groom the backlog (\"what's blocking the auth epic?\", \"estimate these stories\")\n- Ask questions about existing issues, history, and dependencies\n- Trigger actions (status transitions, assignments, linking)\n\n### 2. \"Start Work\" → Drive Flow\nTransform the current button into a full drive execution with live streaming and interactive feedback. The existing \"needs attention\" dashboard badge handles notification when issues are BLOCKED waiting for user input.\n\n### 3. \"Merge Branch\" → Review Flow\nTransform merge into a review-first flow: reviewer agent generates a review summary, user leaves comments, coder addresses them, repeat until approved. Connects to the Code Reviews epic (xpo-6598d3).\n\n### 4. Backend infrastructure\n- API endpoint to start/stop a drive (spawns agent as detached subprocess, returns session ID)\n- SSE endpoint that tails the trail file (streams trail events as they're written)\n- PID tracking in `.xpo/drives/` for lifecycle management and server reconnection\n- Trail recording (xpo-748873) is a prerequisite — it's the data format the SSE stream serves\n- `xpo deliver` CLI command for non-interactive mode\n\n## Impact on existing backlog\n\n### Supercedes (absorbed into this epic)\n\n| Issue | Current parent | Why |\n|-------|---------------|-----|\n| xpo-a168ae \"Spectator mode\" | xpo-748873 | IS the web UI drive flow |\n| xpo-234823 \"Drive from UI — subprocess launch\" | xpo-748873 | IS pillar 4 (API endpoint) |\n| xpo-7a4b19 \"Trail watcher — SSE broadcast\" | xpo-748873 | IS the SSE endpoint |\n| xpo-575923 \"Trail viewer component\" | xpo-748873 | Becomes part of drive flow UI |\n| xpo-eaf666 \"Separate Reviewer agent\" | xpo-6598d3 | IS the Reviewer in the agent hierarchy |\n| xpo-564cdb \"--no-merge → --review flag\" | xpo-6598d3 | Replaced by the Review flow pillar |\n\n### Dependencies (must come before this epic)\n\n| Issue | Why |\n|-------|-----|\n| xpo-ca0f2f \"Trail recording — emit JSONL\" | Can't stream what isn't recorded. Trail format is the SSE data source. |\n| xpo-24cd9b \"Code review and review comments\" | Review comment data model needed for the interactive review flow |\n\n### Complementary (independent, enhances this epic)\n\n| Issue | Relationship |\n|-------|-------------|\n| xpo-f12313 \"Multi-agent executors\" | Executor interface supports different model configs per agent role |\n| xpo-3d7608 \"Drive \u0026 Loop robustness\" | Auto-recovery and sandboxing enhance the drive flow |\n| xpo-07b8f3 \"Custom prompts for agents\" | Becomes config for the agent hierarchy's system prompts |\n| xpo-8204d6 \"Telemetry\" | Relevant for cost tracking surface |\n\n### Effect on xpo-748873 (Drive trails)\n\nAfter absorption, xpo-748873 retains: xpo-ca0f2f (trail recording format), xpo-e3d44b (recap cards), xpo-2f3900 (trail replay). The live/UI sub-issues move here. Consider retitling to \"Drive trails: recording and replay\".\n\n## Decisions\n\n| Question | Decision | Rationale |\n|----------|----------|-----------|\n| Agent backend | Detached subprocess with PID tracking | Simplest model that survives server restarts, supports concurrent drives, and needs no new dependencies. ~50 lines of PID lifecycle code. Containerization becomes a future sub-issue when multi-tenant or scaling demands it. |\n| `xpo loop` in web UI | CLI-only for now | Loop is a power-user automation tool; web UI focuses on the interactive drive flow |\n| Auth / permissions | Authenticated = authorized | SSH key identity is sufficient. Any authenticated user can trigger drives. RBAC is a separate epic if multi-tenant scenarios materialize. |\n| Cost visibility | Surface existing AgentStats in DriveResult and trail events | Token tracking (TokensIn, TokensOut, CostUSD, Duration) already exists per-call in AgentResult and accumulated in AgentStats via the executor. Currently only logged to terminal. Fix: add AgentStats to DriveResult, include cumulative cost in trail events so the SSE stream can show live cost in the web UI. |\n| State management | Split by purpose: `.xpo/drives/\u003cissue-id\u003e/` for drive state (committed), `.xpo/chats/` for chat sessions (gitignored, user-local) | Drive trails are project data — shared, committed as artifacts on completion. Chat sessions are personal scratch — ephemeral, no issue association, pruned by age. Different lifecycles require different storage and git treatment. |\n\n## Related epics\n\n- xpo-748873 — Drive trails (prerequisite: trail format is the SSE data source)\n- xpo-6598d3 — Code Reviews (reviewer agent, review flow)\n- xpo-3d7608 — Drive \u0026 Loop robustness (auto-recovery, sandboxing)\n- xpo-f12313 — Multi-agent executors (sub-agent model configs)\n\n## Not in scope (yet)\n\n- Multi-agent collaboration in the same session\n- Voice interface\n- GitHub/GitLab integration (PR creation, CI status)\n- Sharing chat sessions between users\n- RBAC / permission model\n\nThis epic needs further design work before implementation begins. Filing to capture the vision and architectural decisions."},"created_at":"2026-08-31T13:08:47.89993Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.251 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"xpo-7e5f34","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-582c7f8f-5a67-4c5f-81c4-f2e5412809d0","text":"Already resolved — `AddIssue` in `internal/exponential/add.go:71-94` auto-assigns `sort_order` when the caller doesn't provide one. It finds the max key in the target status group and generates a fractional key after it. Both MCP `add` and CLI `xpo add` flow through `AddIssue`, so both paths get a correct sort key.\n\nThis was added in commit `62ed41e` (the `xpo drive` feature)."},"created_at":"2026-08-31T13:17:55.243063Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.251 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
